menu "Board configuration"

choice
    prompt "Target board type"
    default BOARD_TYPE_SMD4
    help
        Select the pin map for the hardware variant. All supported boards
        use the ES8388 codec.

config BOARD_TYPE_SMD2
    bool "SMD2 gateway (ES8388, ESP32-C6-WROOM-1 pin map)"

config BOARD_TYPE_SMD4
    bool "SMD3/SMD4 gateway (ES8388, ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 pin map)"
    help
        SMD3 and SMD4 share this pin map (SMD4 is a 2-layer re-route of
        SMD3's unchanged schematic -- see docs/hardware.md and
        archive/hardware/ROTARY_PHONE_SMD4.md). Genuinely different GPIO
        assignments from SMD2, not the same map under a different name --
        SMD3/4 moved the MCU carrier from a soldered WROOM-1 module to an
        ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 dev board and remapped I2S and the line-0 SLIC
        control pins accordingly (confirmed against the schematic netlist,
        not assumed).

config BOARD_TYPE_SMD5
    bool "SMD5 gateway, real Espressif module (ES8388, ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 pin map, native USB)"
    help
        Not yet fabricated as of this writing -- see
        archive/hardware/ANALOG_REDIAL_SMD5.md. Same pin map as SMD4 (identical
        SLIC/codec wiring); the only reason this is a separate board type
        from SMD4 is the flash/console story: SMD5's dual MCU-module
        footprint accepts a real Espressif module here, which keeps native
        USB working exactly like SMD2/SMD4.

config BOARD_TYPE_SMD5_CHEAP
    bool "SMD5 gateway, AliExpress clone module (ES8388, ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 pin map, no native USB)"
    help
        Same board and pin map as BOARD_TYPE_SMD5 above -- the only
        difference is which MCU module variant is physically populated in
        SMD5's dual footprint. The clone module swaps GPIO2/GPIO3 with
        GPIO12/GPIO13 relative to the real module (silicon-fixed USB
        peripheral pins on the real module land as ordinary GPIOs on the
        clone, and vice versa -- see archive/hardware/ANALOG_REDIAL_SMD5.md),
        so native USB-Serial-JTAG doesn't reach this board's USB connector
        once assembled. Selects UART0 as the console instead of native USB
        (see sdkconfig.smd5_cheap.defaults) -- flash and monitor over an
        external UART adapter on GPIO16/17/EN/GPIO9, or OTA once a build is
        confirmed good.

endchoice

endmenu
